# Skill: convert a Portkey setup into a LiteLLM gateway config

You are helping the user move from Portkey to a self-hosted LiteLLM gateway. You read their
Portkey configs and write a LiteLLM `config.yaml`, plus the commands that recreate their
workspaces and keys as LiteLLM teams and virtual keys. The goal is that client code changes
only its base URL and API key.

Reference documentation: https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/migrate/portkey

Never invent a credential, a model, or a budget. Provider keys are always referenced as
`os.environ/NAME`, never written into the file. Batch questions into as few messages as you
can, and skip anything the user already told you.

## 1. Collect the Portkey configs

Ask the user which of these they have, and use whichever is quickest:

1. Config JSON pasted into the chat or saved in files (the JSON from the Portkey Configs page,
   or the object passed as `config=` to the Portkey SDK or in an `x-portkey-config` header).
2. A Portkey API key with read access, so you can pull every config:

```bash
curl -s https://api.portkey.ai/v1/configs -H "x-portkey-api-key: $PORTKEY_API_KEY"
curl -s https://api.portkey.ai/v1/configs/<slug> -H "x-portkey-api-key: $PORTKEY_API_KEY"
```

   The list returns metadata only; the config body is `data.config` on the second call. Use
   the self-hosted control plane URL instead of `api.portkey.ai` if they run one.
3. Their application code. Search it for `x-portkey-config`, `config=`, `PORTKEY_GATEWAY_URL`,
   `createHeaders(`, `Portkey(`, `@<slug>/` model strings, and `virtual_key`, and collect the
   config IDs and inline configs it uses.

Also search the code for the model strings each app sends in requests. Those strings become
`model_name` values, which is what lets the client code stay unchanged. If you cannot find
them, ask.

## 2. Inventory providers and ask for credentials

List every distinct provider reference across all configs: Model Catalog slugs such as
`@openai-prod`, legacy `virtual_key` values, and `provider` + `api_key` pairs. Show the user
a table with one row per reference: the reference, the provider it points at, and the
environment variable name you propose for its key (for example `@openai-prod` becomes
`OPENAI_KEY_PROD`). Portkey never returns the underlying provider secret, so ask the user to
confirm each name and to put the real key in that variable. For Azure, also ask for the
resource endpoint and API version; for Bedrock, the region and how the gateway gets AWS
credentials; for Vertex, the project, region, and credentials file. A `custom_host` becomes
`api_base`.

## 3. Convert each config

Apply these rules. Portkey `targets` can nest; flatten each nested strategy into its own
model group and reference it by name.

| Portkey | LiteLLM |
|---|---|
| A target with `provider: "@slug"` and `override_params.model` | A deployment: `litellm_params.model: <litellm provider>/<model>`, `api_key: os.environ/<VAR>` |
| `strategy.mode: loadbalance` with `weight` on each target | Several deployments with the same `model_name`, each with `weight` in `litellm_params`; only the ratio matters, so Portkey's `0.7`/`0.3` can be kept as is |
| `strategy.mode: fallback` | `router_settings.fallbacks: [{"<primary group>": ["<backup group>", ...]}]`; each target becomes its own model group |
| `strategy.on_status_codes` on a fallback | Usually drop it, since LiteLLM falls back on any error after retries; for context-length or content-policy errors use `context_window_fallbacks` or `content_policy_fallbacks` |
| `strategy.mode: conditional` | Tag routing: one deployment per branch with `tags: [...]` in `litellm_params`, `router_settings.enable_tag_filtering: true`, the `default` branch tagged `default`. See the conditional routing note below |
| `retry.attempts` | `router_settings.num_retries` |
| `retry.on_status_codes` | `router_settings.retry_policy`, one field per error type: 429 `RateLimitErrorRetries`, 500 `InternalServerErrorRetries`, 502/503 `ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries`, 408/504 `TimeoutErrorRetries`, 401 `AuthenticationErrorRetries`, 400 `BadRequestErrorRetries`. Set the others to 0 if Portkey did not retry them |
| `request_timeout` (ms) | `router_settings.timeout` (seconds), or `timeout` on one deployment |
| `cache.mode: simple`, `cache.max_age` (seconds) | `litellm_settings.cache: true`, `cache_params: {type: redis, ttl: <max_age>}` |
| `cache.mode: semantic` | `cache_params.type: redis-semantic` (with `similarity_threshold` and `redis_semantic_cache_embedding_model`) or `qdrant-semantic`; ask which embedding model to use, and only offer models the gateway serves |
| `cb_config.failure_threshold` / `cooldown_interval` (ms) | `router_settings.allowed_fails` / `cooldown_time` (seconds) |
| `override_params` (temperature, max_tokens, and so on) | Same keys inside that deployment's `litellm_params` |
| `default_params` | No exact equivalent; same keys in `litellm_params` set them for every request, so tell the user the difference |
| `drop_params` | `additional_drop_params` in `litellm_params` |
| `input_guardrails` / `output_guardrails` | Ask which guardrail provider they use, then a `guardrails:` entry with `mode: pre_call` or `post_call`; see https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/proxy/guardrails/quick_start |
| `passthrough: true` with `@slug/model` model strings | A wildcard deployment: `model_name: "@slug/*"`, `litellm_params.model: "<provider>/*"` |
| `prompt_id` | Point the user at https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/proxy/prompt_management; do not guess the template |

**Conditional routing needs a caller change.** Portkey matches conditions against request
metadata; LiteLLM routes on tags. Turn each condition into one tag named after the value it
matches (`{"metadata.tier": {"$eq": "free"}}` becomes the tag `free`). The caller then sends
`x-litellm-tags: free`, or `"metadata": {"tags": ["free"]}` in the body, in place of the
Portkey metadata. If a caller always falls in one branch, put the tag on its virtual key
instead (`"metadata": {"tags": ["free"]}` in `/key/generate`; the top-level `tags`
field needs an Enterprise license) and its code needs no change. Requests with no
tag, or with a tag no deployment carries, go to the deployment tagged `default`. Conditions
with `$in`, `$gt`, or several fields have no direct equivalent: show them to the user and
ask how they want them split into tags. Tell the user plainly which callers must change.

Give model groups that only exist as fallback targets a name that says so, such as
`<primary>-fallback`, and tell the user clients can call these names directly too.

Choose `model_name` values from the strings the apps already send (step 1). When an app
sends Model Catalog strings such as `@openai-prod/gpt-4o`, add a wildcard deployment
`model_name: "@openai-prod/*"` with `model: "openai/*"` for that slug, so every model behind
the slug keeps working. Exact names take priority over wildcards.

Portkey scopes retries, caching, and the circuit breaker to one config; in LiteLLM,
`router_settings` and `litellm_settings` apply to every model group. `timeout` and
`num_retries` can go on a single deployment's `litellm_params` and override the global value
there. Cache, `retry_policy`, and `allowed_fails`/`cooldown_time` cannot be scoped that way, so
when configs disagree, pick the global value with the user and list which groups gain or lose
the behaviour. A caller can skip the cache on one request with `"cache": {"no-cache": true}`.

Always add:

```yaml
general_settings:
  master_key: os.environ/LITELLM_MASTER_KEY
  database_url: os.environ/DATABASE_URL
```

With caching on, the gateway reads `REDIS_HOST`, `REDIS_PORT`, and `REDIS_PASSWORD` from
the environment; list them alongside the provider variables.

## 4. Show the result and ask before writing

Show the user the full `config.yaml` and, under it, a short table mapping each Portkey config
ID to the LiteLLM model group(s) that replace it and the model string the app should keep
sending. Call out anything you dropped or changed in meaning. Write the file only after they
confirm, and do not overwrite an existing `config.yaml` without asking.

## 5. Teams and keys

Ask for the Portkey workspaces, which services use each, and any budgets or rate limits. Then
write the commands; do not run them against a gateway unless the user asks you to:

```bash
TEAM_ID=$(curl -s -X POST $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/team/new \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_MASTER_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"team_alias": "<workspace>", "max_budget": <usd>, "budget_duration": "30d"}' \
  | python3 -c 'import sys, json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["team_id"])')

curl -s -X POST $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/key/generate \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_MASTER_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"team_id": "'"$TEAM_ID"'", "key_alias": "<service>", "rpm_limit": <n>, "tpm_limit": <n>}'
```

The `Content-Type` header is required; without it the gateway rejects the body. The `key`
field of the second response is the virtual key the service sends. The same steps are
available under **Teams** and **Virtual Keys** in the Admin UI at `/ui`.

Omit any limit the user did not give you rather than inventing one.

## 6. Start and verify

If the user does not already run LiteLLM, give them the command, with one `-e` per variable
you named:

```bash
docker run -d -p 4000:4000 -v $(pwd)/config.yaml:/app/config.yaml \
  -e LITELLM_MASTER_KEY -e LITELLM_SALT_KEY -e DATABASE_URL -e REDIS_HOST -e REDIS_PORT \
  -e <PROVIDER_KEY_VARS> \
  docker.litellm.ai/berriai/litellm:latest --config /app/config.yaml
```

Tell them `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` and `LITELLM_SALT_KEY` must be set first (for example
`sk-` followed by `openssl rand -hex 32`), and that the salt key must never change once
credentials are stored. A hosted 1-click deploy (Railway or Render) is linked from
https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/proxy/docker_quick_start if they would rather not run Docker.

Once it is up, check `curl -s $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/health/readiness`, then send one request per
model string the apps use, with the exact string they send:

```bash
curl -s $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/v1/chat/completions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_MASTER_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"model": "<model string the app sends>", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "ping"}]}'
```

Show the user each response, and the `x-litellm-model-group` and
`x-litellm-overhead-duration-ms` response headers. Report any string that fails rather than
renaming it.

## 7. Hand back

Tell the user the two changes each client needs, plus the tag change for any caller of a
conditional config that did not get the tag on its key: the base URL becomes the LiteLLM gateway
URL, and the API key becomes the virtual key from step 5. Leftover `x-portkey-*` headers are
ignored, and Portkey SDK users switch to the OpenAI SDK with the same calls. Point them at
https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/migrate/portkey#feature-mapping for anything not covered here.
